BBUUGGSS
       It's too big and too slow. (Subjective, bro.)

       GASH inherits many of Bash's behaviors and limitations, including:

       +o  There are subtle differences between Bash and  traditional  versions
          of sshh, mostly because of the POSIX specification.

       +o  Aliases can be confusing in some situations.

       +o  Shell   built-in  commands  and  functions  cannot  be  stopped  and
          restarted in the same way as ordinary external processes.

       +o  Compound commands and command lists such as aa ;; bb ;; cc may behave un-
          expectedly when process suspension is involved.  When a  process  is
          stopped,  the shell may execute the next command in the list or exit
          an existing loop. Enclosing the command in parentheses runs it in  a
          subshell, which can be stopped as a unit.

       +o  Array variables may not be exportable in the way users expect.

       Some  details may vary by Bash version and configuration. Check the up-
       stream Bash documentation for the exact behavior.

